## State Machine Diagram

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Refraction_Active: mode = refraction, healthy environment
    Refraction_Active --> Layered_Fallback: A motion signal appears (0ms)
    Layered_Fallback --> Refraction_Recovering: Signals quiet for 140ms
    Refraction_Recovering --> Layered_Fallback: Motion during the recovery
    Refraction_Recovering --> Refraction_Active: After 287ms (the rim fades back 60 + 220ms later)
    Refraction_Active --> Blur_Degraded: Power saving, or frame budget reduced
    Blur_Degraded --> Refraction_Active: Power saving off and frame budget ok again
    Blur_Degraded --> Fallback_Mode: A motion signal appears (0ms, switches to fallbackMode)
    Fallback_Mode --> Settling: Signals quiet for 140ms
    Settling --> Fallback_Mode: Motion again
    Settling --> Blur_Degraded: After 299ms
    Refraction_Active --> Flat: Frame budget minimal
    Blur_Degraded --> Flat: Frame budget minimal
    Flat --> Blur_Degraded: A later probe reads reduced, or ok with power saving on
    Flat --> Refraction_Active: A later probe reads ok, with power saving off
```
A surface whose `mode` is `blur`, `glass` or `acrylic` keeps its own material at rest and goes round the same `Fallback_Mode` and `Settling` loop in motion. So a moving surface never drags a misaligned backdrop along, and it returns to full refraction once it stops.
